Two files written:

**`src/components/ErrorBoundary.tsx`** — the component itself:
- `getDerivedStateFromError` captures the error into state, triggering the fallback render
- `componentDidCatch` logs `error` + `componentStack` to console and fires the optional `onError` prop
- `retry()` resets state, re-mounting children
- Stack trace details panel is only shown in non-production environments
- Accepts a `fallback` render-prop for full custom UI control

**`src/components/ErrorBoundary.test.tsx`** — covers the four key behaviors:

| Test | What it checks |
|---|---|
| No error | Children render normally |
| Default fallback | Alert shown with error message |
| Retry button | Clears error, re-renders children |
| `onError` prop | Called with `Error` + `ErrorInfo` |
| Custom fallback | Render-prop receives error + retry fn |

**Usage:**

```tsx
// Default fallback
<ErrorBoundary onError={(err, info) => reportToSentry(err, info)}>
  <MyWidget />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback UI
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(err, retry) => (
  <div>Oops: {err.message} <button onClick={retry}>Retry</button></div>
)}>
  <MyWidget />
</ErrorBoundary>
```
